
$theme {}

$title { Lyapunov Fractals }

$og_image { /media/lyapunov/preview.png }

$og_description { Exploring and explaining Mario Markus's logistic Lyapunov fractals }

$css {
    .gallery img {
        width: calc(max(25vw, 64px) - 0.5em);
        height: calc(max(25vw, 64px) - 0.5em);
        padding: 0;
        border-radius: 0.25rem;
        border: 0.25rem solid transparent;
    }

    .gallery img:hover {
        border: 0;
        clip-path: inset(0) !important;
    }

    .gallery {
        margin-top: 0.5rem;
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        align-content: flex-start;
        gap: 0px;
        width: fit-content;
    }

    .blog-content {
        padding-left: 1em;
        padding-right: 1em;

        height: 100%;
        overflow-y: scroll;
    }

    .interactive-plot {
        width: 100%;
        height: 500px;
    }

    @media (min-width: 600px) {
        .gallery img {
            width: calc(max(min(25vw, 512px), 256px) - 0.875em);
            height: calc(max(min(25vw, 512px), 256px) - 0.875em);
        }

        .gallery {
            gap: 0.5em;
        }

    }

    @media (max-width: 768px) {

        .interactive-plot {
            height: 85vh;
            border-bottom: 3px double var(--main-solid);
            border-top: 3px double var(--main-solid);
        }
    }

    .center {
        display: block;
        margin: auto;
        width: fit-content;
    }

    .gallery img:not(.loaded) {
        filter: url(#thumb-blur);
        clip-path: inset(0.25rem);
    }

    #lightbox {
        display: none;
        position: fixed;
        inset: 0;
        background: #000a;
        place-items: center;
        z-index: 2;
    }

    #lightbox.open {
        display: grid;
    }

    #lightbox img {
        max-width: 98vw;
        max-height: 98vh;
        object-fit: contain;
    }

    .render-container {
        height: 75%;
    }

    .carousel {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: row;
        width: 100%;
        overflow-x: scroll;
        gap: 1em;
    }

    .carousel img {
        max-height: 35vh;
        flex-shrink: 0;
    }

    .info-box {
        margin: auto;
        border: 1px solid var(--main-solid);
        background-color: var(--main-faded);
        padding-left: 1em;
        width: fit-content;
    }
}

[id=lightbox| onclick=this.classList.remove('open')] {
    img[id=lightbox-img]{}
}

svg[style=height:0;width:0;margin:0;padding:0;display:block] {
  filter[id=thumb-blur] {
    feGaussianBlur[stdDeviation=3 | edgeMode=duplicate]{}
  }
}

[class=blog-content]{

h1[class=center | style=font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1rem; overflow: visible; ] {The Logistic Lyapunov Fractal}

h2[class=center | style=font-size: 0.7rem; ]{⸺ ooo ⸺}

$layout/loadwall { Load interactive renderer? (designed for desktop) | gpu/lyapunov | render-container }




h3[style=text-decoration:underline]{Overview}

The a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyapunov_fractal]{Lyapunov fractal},
named after a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aleksandr_Lyapunov]{Aleksandr Lyapunov}, was
introduced by a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mario_Markus]{Mario Markus} and
a[href=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/anie.200390466]{Benno Hess} in their 1989
paper a[href=https://doi.org/10.1016/0097-8493(89)90019-8]{Lyapunov exponents of the logistic
map with periodic forcing} (a[href=https://sci-hub.ru/10.1016/0097-8493(89)90019-8]{pirate link}).

The fractal is a visualization of the behavior of the following iterative formula:

$math/math {
    ident r x n A B R:ℝ S s m:M i
    op ( ) + - = · in:∈ ll:{ rr:} %:mod , ...
    text given:"given a sequence of real numbers" where:~ let:~ and:~ values:~ then:"then"
    in
    given ll r_0 , r_1 , r_2 , ... r_n rr
    let x_0 = 0.5 ,
    then x_{n+1} = r_n · x_n · {(1 - x_n)}
}

The function $math/math { inline auto f{(x)} = r dot x dot {(1 - x)}} is known as the a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logistic_map]{logistic map}. We won't dwell overly long on the question of why one should care about this formula. There are plenty of answers one might find more or less compelling, but let's just put the cart before the horse and say that the map is interesting because interest in it leads to interesting fractals.

Let's try and get a feel for what this map is, beginning with some simple plots:

[class=interactive-plot] { $math/plot { logistic_3d } }

[class=info-box] { In interactive plots, the bounds of the sliders have been set somewhat conservatively, for the sake of readers who may be using low-end devices. However, most of these limits are not enforced for the textual input elements. You may push your device to its limits, at your own peril. }


Playing around with the parameters a bit, one can observe a number of basic facts: Since 0 and 1 get mapped to 0 regardless of the value of r, those make for uninteresting starting values. For small values of r, the unit interval (excluding the endpoints) converges on a single value. As r increases, at some point there's a change, and each point on the interval eventually finds itself bouncing between two values -- the i{same} two values, across the whole interval. As r increases further, there are more values to bounce between, and by the time r is quite close to 4, the plot gets very messy very quickly and ceases to be a useful visualization.

The most important takeaway from that visualization is that, as long as it's not 0 or 1, we could start from any x-value and the system's long-term dynamics would be qualitatively similar. So, let's not keep wasting a dimension of our visualization on that. Instead, let's take a i{single} $math/math { inline auto x_0 } and plot its behavior for many timesteps:

[class=interactive-plot] { $math/plot { logistic/timeseries } }

We can see here that indeed the long-term qualitative behavior of the system is not greatly impacted by our choice of $math/math { inline auto x_0 }. What stands out now is that there are values of $math/math {inline auto r} for which the long-term behavior is periodic, bouncing between the same several values in a repetitious pattern, and there are other values where it appears more i{chaotic}, albeit still bounded to some range or ranges. If you turn up the iteration count, you'll see that plot becomes qualitatively monotonous in the long run in either case. So, plotting the entire timeseries is still a bit of a waste of a dimension in our visualization of this system. Perhaps we should sweep across values of $math/math {inline auto r} and view some kind of summary of the long-term behavior of the system under that parametrization.

todo: the classic logistic histogram sweep :)

Now we're getting somewhere. There is a lot going on here. This kind of figure is known as a bifurcation diagram, and the bifurcation diagram of the logistic map has been studied i{extensively}. There's a good chance you've seen it before, maybe you've even seen a demonstration of how it relates to the Mandelbrot set. It is tempting to attempt to catalogue every interesting fact and feature of the bifurcation diagram, but this is an article about i{Lyapunov fractals}, so let's stay on task.

In the context of our journey toward understanding the Lyapunov fractal, we are interested in a single simple question about each parametrization: is the trajectory stable, or chaotic? In a sense, the bifurcation diagram is telling us i{too much}. We'd like to put a single number to each parametrization, characterizing just how tidy or untidy a system it defined. We can look at any slice of the bifurcation diagram and eyeball it, but how can we i{compute} it?

Well, one idea would be to simply take the limit of the average of the logarithm of the magnitude of the derivative:

$math/math { auto lambda = underover lim {n arrowr inf} {} end 1/n underover sum {i=1} {n} end log{|d/{d x} f_i{(x_i)}|} }

:)

Many of the explanations you can find online of the Lyapunov fractal would be happy to just drop this formula on you from on high and move on, but personally I think you'd be justified in demanding a little more explanation than that.

The idea from which this measure (the a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyapunov_exponent]{maximal Lyapunov exponent (MLE)}) arises is that of defining the quantity we're after as i{sensitivity to initial conditions}. If we give the system a tiny nudge at the start, how much do we expect that to change the result, hundreds of iterations later? If you return to the previous visualizations and toy with them for a bit, you can see that while the long term behavior of the system is i{qualitatively} largely independent of $math/math {inline auto x_0}, this idea of how sensitive the i{specific values} at any point late in the sequence are to slight changes in $math/math {inline auto x_0} lines up well with our looser intuitive notions of apparent-order-versus-apparent-chaos.

So then we just need to quantify this sensitivity. Let's consider just one iteration. How sensitive is $math/math { inline auto x_1 = f(x_0) } to a change in $math/math { inline auto x_0 }? Well, this is exactly what a derivative $math/math {inline auto d/{d x_0} x_1 = d/{d x_0} f(x_0)} tells us.

Now, how about $math/math { inline auto x_2 }? Well, we have $math/math { inline auto x_2 = f(x_1) = f(f(x_1)) } and thus $math/math { inline auto d/{d x_0} x_2 = d/{d x_0} f(x_1) = d/{d x_0} f(f(x_1)) }. This is computed via the chain rule TODO link and continue explanation

In the original paper, the sequence has only two distinct r-values, denoted A and B, and is given as a short string like "AB", "BBABA", &c, which repeats indefinitely. The fractal, then, is a parameter sweep: each pixel's x-coordinate determines its A-value, and the y-coordinate determines B. The pixel's color is determined by measuring the long-term stability of the system under that parametrization.

The stability measure employed is the a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyapunov_exponent]{maximal Lyapunov exponent (MLE)}, and this is where the fractal gets its name (although, since Aleksandr Lyapunov never lived to see these fractals, they are sometimes called "Markus-Lyapunov fractals" instead). In the case of this discrete system, the idea is to take the average of the logarithm of the magnitude of the derivative. We then take a negative MLE to indicate stability or convergence, while a positive MLE indicates chaos. Often, these cases are colorized separately. The values are unbounded, so some normalization is required.

Usually when one says "Lyapunov fractal", it is in reference to the system just described, based on the logistic map. However, Lyapunov exponents can be computed for many different systems, and similar visualizations of those systems can just as well be called Lyapunov fractals. Indeed, Markus himself would go on to expand on the idea in a 1990 publication, a[href=https://doi.org/10.1063/1.4822940]{Chaos in Maps with Continuous and Discontinuous Maxima}, in which he explored two interesting modifications to the system, one of which was to use a different, discontinuous map:

$math/math {
    ident alpha:~ x r n
    text if:~
    op > leq:≤ + ( ) - = ·
    in
    x_{n+1} = r_n · x_n · {(1 - x_n)} if x_n > 0.5
    x_{n+1} = r_n · x_n · {(1 - x_n)} + 0.25 {(alpha - 1)} {(r_n - 2)} if x_n leq 0.5
}

Where α is a real parameter, typically just a little less than 1. Note that in the case where α is 1, this reduces to the familiar logistic map.

Note that in the discontinuous case, Markus chose to set the starting x value to 0.499 instead of 0.5. The other idea he introduced was to apply a little (pseudo-)random variation to the sequence of A's and B's. The exact details of how that variation is implemented are a little bit hard to parse, in my opinion, but I believe I have settled on an implementation which closely matches the results of Markus's paper. TODO describe this explain the parameter

Most explanations of the Lyapunov fractal on the web (at time of writing) don't explore these variations, and I think that's a shame; which is one of the primary reasons I decided to write this whole article in the first place.

A somewhat more common modification is to include a third value, C, corresponding either to a third spatial dimension or time. a[href=https://gidden.net/2017/03/09/exploring-lyapunov-space/]{Tom Gidden's 2017 blog post} provides a more in-depth description of that, and a[href=https://www.youtube.com/shorts/KAK6IJMbhwI]{this short animation by @desden0va on youtube} makes the idea intuitive.

h3[style=text-decoration:underline]{Implementations}

a[href=https://tangled.org/ponder.ooo/sketches/blob/main/lyapunov.py]{The code used to render these fractals} is publicly available. It is written as a a[href=https://tangled.org/ponder.ooo/snakepyt]{snakepyt} sketch, not a normal python script, and is a bit messy, since it's a snapshot of the working copy. However, it's only about 200 lines, and the logic is a fairly straightforward encoding of the methods described here.



h3[style=text-decoration:underline]{Other Results}

The original paper concerns itself only with A and B values in the range [0,4]. However, there is no technical constraint that prevents us from peeking outside that box. When we do, it becomes visually apparent that this range is not entirely arbitrary (a[href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logistic_map#Characterization_of_the_logistic_map]{the logistic map remains finite there}). The crisp edge of this "blessed" square stands out prominently. Nevertheless, there is some interesting structure outside of it. Some of the superstable curves poke out into the void of divergence. A smaller, distorted square exists where A and B are both negative. There are even isolated islands of stability, i{apparently} disconnected from the rest. i{Apparently} is emphasized as this is only a visual assessment of a finite-resolution render.

h3[style=text-decoration:underline]{Presets}

Presets in the interactive renderer have been set up to approximately reproduce the figures from Markus's 1989 and 1990 papers. The renders are not 100% faithful, but they are best-effort. The papers specify the r-value sequences and the coordinate bounds of each render. They do not specify the starting offset in the sequence, which, as I discovered in the course of implementing the presets, is not always 0. This page's interactive renderer has a variable width and height, so I calculated a center point and zoom value that would approximately match the original bounds. It may be necessary to resize one's window, zoom, and pan around, in order to get a more exact match to what is present in the original papers. Some of the original renders also exhibit anisotropic stretching. My renderer does not at present have the capacity to replicate this effect. The original papers specify the $math/math {inline auto x_0} values for many of the figures, but I found that some visual features (for example, in 1989 Fig. 1(a)) could not be reproduced without modifying $math/math {inline auto x_0} slightly. I opted to make this modification, prioritizing visual similarity with the original visualization over precise copying of parameters.


h3[style=text-decoration:underline]{Algebraic Curves}

Qualitatively, the fractal appears to be structured along a "skeleton" of precise curves, the so-called "superstable" curves, where the Lyapunov exponent diverges to $math/math { inline auto -inf }. We can actually produce exact algebraic descriptions of these curves!

Consider the formula for the Lyapunov exponent: $math/math { inline auto underover lim {n arrowr inf} {} end 1/n underover sum {i=1} {n} end log{|d/{d x} f_i{(x_i)}|} }

Since $math/math { inline auto log{|x|} } diverges to $math/math { inline auto -inf } as x approaches 0, we have that the entire sum must diverge when $math/math { inline auto d/{d x} f_n{(x_n)} = 0 }. Given $math/math { inline auto x_0 = 1/2} we can apply the logistic formula with alternating parameters $math/math {inline auto a} and $math/math {inline auto b} repeatedly and obtain $math/math { inline auto d/{d x} f_n{(x_n)} } as a multivariate polynomial in $math/math {inline auto a} and $math/math {inline auto b}. I'd go as far as to suggest that the set of points where the Lyapunov exponent diverges toward negative infinity is precisely the union of the zero sets of all these polynomials. I suggest it instead of asserting it because I suspect that a proof might require irritatingly subtle considerations.

Because $math/math { inline auto x_0 = 1/2 }, the first iteration of our iterative process actually has derivative 0 uniformly across all r-values, which is why the sum in our Lyapunov exponent calculation begins at index 1 instead of 0. Anyway, assuming the basic "AB" sequence, and choosing scaling factors $math/math { inline auto c_n } so as to produce polynomials with the same zeros but with integer coefficients, the first few curves $math/math { inline auto P_n = c_n dot d/{d x} f_n{(x_n)} = 0 } are as follows:

$math/math {auto P_1 = {- a} + 2 = 0

P_2 = a^2 b - 4 ab + 8 = 0

P_3 = a^5b^2 - 8 a^4b^2 + 16 a^3 b^2 + 16 a^3 b - 64 a^2 b + 128 = 0

P_4 = a^10b^5 - 16a^9b^5 + 96a^8b^5 + 32a^8b^4 - 256a^7b^5 - 384a^7b^4 + 256a^6b^5 + 1536a^6b^4 + 256a^6b^3 - 2048a^5b^4 - 1792a^5b^3 + 2048a^4b^3 + 4096a^3b^3 + 4096a^3b^2 - 16384a^2b^2 + 32768 = 0
}

While the first few of these can be worked out by hand (an exercise for the reader, of course), they grow in complexity very quickly. By the sixth iteration, there are already hundreds of terms, and by the tenth, a plaintext file containing the formula takes up several megabytes. I had an LLM agent use sympy to produce the first ten iterations of polynomials, and while I didn't look at the code at all, the task was straightforward and the plots look correct:

[class=carousel] {
    img[src=/media/lyapunov/full/curve_composite.webp] {}
    img[src=/media/lyapunov/full/curve_d01.webp] {}
    img[src=/media/lyapunov/full/curve_d02.webp] {}
    img[src=/media/lyapunov/full/curve_d03.webp] {}
    img[src=/media/lyapunov/full/curve_d04.webp] {}
    img[src=/media/lyapunov/full/curve_d05.webp] {}
    img[src=/media/lyapunov/full/curve_d06.webp] {}
    img[src=/media/lyapunov/full/curve_d07.webp] {}
    img[src=/media/lyapunov/full/curve_d08.webp] {}
    img[src=/media/lyapunov/full/curve_d09.webp] {}
    img[src=/media/lyapunov/full/curve_d10.webp] {}
}

You can see how these curves accumulate and form the "backbone" of the fractal in the interactive renderer by setting "skip" to 1 and "iterations" to 2, and then gradually incrementing the iteration count. The negative-infinite exponents pleasingly trace out exactly these algebraic curves. It's also interesting to note the alternation between two different qualitative types of curve on even and odd iterations. I have not put much thought into that, but I'm sure there's an interesting reason for it.

Of course, in the original papers, the first 600 iterations are skipped. Some of the algebraic curves from early on are lost due to this, but others reassert themselves no matter how late the accumulation begins. Indeed even with "skip" set to 1 in the renderer you can see that many of the lines appear to fade away quite quickly as the iteration count grows. In reality the limit does still diverge at every point on any of these curves, it's just that the ones that fade have vanishing influence on nearby points. The curves that very i{visibly} remain are those that are included in infinitely many later curves. If it is only these "limiting superstable curves" one cares about, however, there is no finite number of iterations we can skip to exclude all the rest. But I don't feel like pursuing that line of thought any further here and now.

Anyway, I think this is a pretty neat decomposition of much of the structure of the fractal.

